    "product_id": "20741097-2c20-4236-b92f-c2c67662accd",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 150840\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n140 AM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry weather and warming temperatures starting today, and lasting\n  through Monday.\n\n- Unsettled weather will return Tuesday and again late week, \n  leading to a couple periods with rain chances over South Central\n  Arizona and cooling temperatures near to below daily normals.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nThe shortwave that moved across northern AZ yesterday is now\npositioned along the boarder between NM and TX. Behind the \nshortwave, to our region's west, upper level ridging is building \nand will quickly move eastwards and strengthen over the Desert SW \nstarting today.That being said, H5 heights will climb back up \nto between 577-580 dam by this afternoon, which will diminish any\nfurther rainfall and begin to trend temperatures back into the \nmid to upper 70s across the lower deserts. Further warming will \noccur heading into Monday where temperatures will increase to the \nupper 70s with the Phoenix Metro having near an 80% chance of \nhitting at least 80F.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nBy late monday night/early Tuesday morning another upper level\ntrough will push inland with models now showing the core of this\nnext system lifting to the NE through NV and UT. However the\nbottom of the trough will move through Arizona providing enough\nascent to the region to increase PoPs back to between 60-90% \nacross South-Central AZ, and 20-60% across SW AZ. PWATs Tuesday \nwill also increase to over 200%, with the latest WPC QPF \nindicating Tuesday's rain totals between 0.25\"-0.50\" across the \nGreater Phoenix area, locally higher in thunderstorms, and close \nto 1.00\" over Central AZ higher terrain to the north/northeast. \nHowever, considerable spread remains in the model guidance, with \n25th-75th percentile QPF ranging between a few tenths to upwards \nof 1.25\", focused over the higher terrain to the north/northeast \nof Phoenix. A 1-2 day break in the unsettled weather will then \nlikely ensue following Tuesday, ahead of the next system. \n\nDuring the latter half of the week, ensembles are pointing \ntowards yet another upper level trough moving inland from the \nPacific NW, however, there are still considerable discrepancies in\nthe trajectory of this followup system (how far south it will \npropagate, which will impact the moisture return and placement of\nthe best upper level ascent mechanisms). Due to this, confidence \nremains low for PoPs/QPF late in the work week, but at the very \nleast, there is building consensus for an even cooler airmass to \nenter the region as a result of this second system. Latest NBM \nhighs continue to trend towards the 60s to lower 70s by late week.\nHowever, the spread in the model guidance increases considerably \non Friday, with IQRs on the NBM forecast temperatures increasing \nto 8F-10F. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0525Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo aviation concerns are anticipated through the forecast period\nunder mostly clear skies. Winds will maintain a light easterly \ncomponent overnight and through much of the day Sunday. There is \nlow confidence that a westerly shift will occur at KPHX Sunday \nafternoon. Speeds should remain aob 6 kts through the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather concerns will exist through the forecast\nperiod. Winds will favor a W at KIPL overnight before switching\nout of the SE by Sunday afternoon. At KBLH, a N-NE wind direction\nwill be common. Extended periods of calm and vrb conditions will\nbe likely at both terminals, especially after sunrise Sunday \nmorning. FEW to SCT low clouds with bases aoa 7 kt will persist \nover SE California. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry conditions and temperatures warming near to slightly above \nnormal are expected starting today lasting through Monday before \nanother weather system moves through the region on Tuesday.\nDespite the upcoming drier and warmer conditions afternoon \nminimum humidities will continue to range between 30-60% areawide\nthrough the week, following good to excellent overnight recovery.\nWidespread breezy and locally windy conditions in Southeast CA \nwill develop with the next system on Tuesday. This system will \nalso cool temperatures back into a below normal category. The next\nweather system will likely reach the area by Friday and result in\neven cooler temperatures and another round of rain chances. \n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area through at least early next week due to \nwater released from the dam. Water releases remain at 3000 cfs \nand are expected to remain at this level through the next several\ndays. This will have the potential to flood farm fields and low- \nwater crossings along the river downstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Whittock/Ryan \nAVIATION...Salerno \nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock/Ryan \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero\n",
    "risk": "MEDIUM",
